# ord-labeled-v1 — premise-LABEL citation policy for worked example S7
#
# S6's ord-named-v1 with one addition and one new rule. The addition is that
# both rules LABEL their premises (grammar App. B.4): a label names the premise
# SLOT, in the rule that declares it, independently of whatever term ends up
# filling that slot in any particular instance.
#
# S7's subject — the lara-syntax@0.8 premise-label citation spelling
# (`(prem <label>)`, #131) — is the one part of it that reaches THIS file.
# That is the difference from S6, whose subject lived entirely in the
# artifact: `@0.6` named a slot by the source name of the term filling it, so
# nothing about it was policy-visible; `@0.8` lets a certificate cite the
# rule's own declared name for the slot, and a rule can only be cited that way
# if it declares the labels. Everything else is unchanged — labels never enter
# `Unit` (`stripPremiseLabels`), so the compiled policy is byte-for-byte the
# one this file would compile to with the labels deleted.
#
# Two rules, because #131 exists for a case one rule cannot show:
#
#   lt_recheck — S6's rule, labelled. Its two slots are filled by two DIFFERENT
#                leaves, so `@0.6`'s leaf names already worked here; the labels
#                are the second, equivalent spelling.
#
#   le_reflex  — the case `@0.6` cannot express. Its two slots take the same
#                premise pattern, so ONE leaf can fill both, and then the leaf's
#                name is ambiguous between slot 0 and slot 1 — the elaborator
#                rejects it as `CertSlotMultiSlot` and the author is forced back
#                to numerals. `left` and `right` name the slots themselves, so
#                they stay unambiguous no matter what fills them.
#
# No bridge rule and no measurand: like S6, S7's claims ARE the bare
# comparisons, so the policy needs only the rules that certify them. S2 carries
# the §3.6 layering story.

policy ord-labeled-v1

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2:
# every predicate and constructor symbol this policy's rules and programs may use, with the sort
# of each argument position. `Num` and `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric
# and string literals) and are not declarable.
#
# Generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` (union-find over argument occurrences, finest consistent
# partition), then the sorts were given their intended names — the one editorial step, and the
# only one:
#
#   cabal exec -- runghc --ghc-arg=-package --ghc-arg=lara scripts/infer-sigma.hs \
#     examples/S7/ord-labeled-v1.policy.lara examples/S7/example.lara

sort Experiment, Cell, System, Measurand, Dataset

con accuracy : Measurand
con exp1 : Experiment
con imagenet_val : Dataset
con score_cell(System, Measurand, Dataset, Num) : Cell
con sys_base : System
con sys_new : System

pred num_le(Num, Num)
pred num_lt(Num, Num)
pred reports(Experiment, Cell)

# --- The certificate-checked strict comparison, with labelled slots ---------------------------
# S6's lt_recheck verbatim, except that each premise now carries a label. Slot order is still
# the premise list order — slot 0 is the baseline cell, slot 1 the system cell — and the labels
# `base` and `new` are the rule's own names for those two slots. S7's artifact cites them.
#
# Both compared values trace to a consulted premise (design §2.4): a threshold or public
# baseline is not a bare goal constant, it enters as a leaf feeding a premise slot.

rule lt_recheck(S, B, Q, D, Exp, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ base: reports(Exp, score_cell(B, Q, D, Bv)),
                 new:  reports(Exp, score_cell(S, Q, D, Sv)) ]
  conclusion = num_lt(Bv, Sv)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (ord@1, sha256:ord-labeled-v1-theory-0) ]

# --- The reflexive case: two slots, one premise pattern ---------------------------------------
# Both premises are the SAME shape, so a single leaf can fill both — and that is exactly the
# instance a `@0.6` name cannot cite: `(prem base_cell)` would occupy slots 0 and 1 at once.
# `left` and `right` are the rule's names for the two slots, so they stay unambiguous however
# the instance is filled. ord@1 decides the resulting num_le at the tie the same way it decides
# S3's (0.71 <= 0.71), from the two cited cells' numerals.
#
# The rule is deliberately reflexive-friendly rather than reflexive-only: nothing constrains S1
# and S2 to be equal, so the same rule also compares two different systems. The artifact happens
# to instantiate it at the tie, which is where one leaf fills both slots.

rule le_reflex(S1, S2, Q, D, Exp, Lv, Rv)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ left:  reports(Exp, score_cell(S1, Q, D, Lv)),
                 right: reports(Exp, score_cell(S2, Q, D, Rv)) ]
  conclusion = num_le(Lv, Rv)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (ord@1, sha256:ord-labeled-v1-theory-0) ]

# The empty theory the ord@1 certificates cite: the certified comparisons are pure arithmetic, so
# the theory contributes no entries — the digest is selection identity only (the S1 precedent).
# Emptiness here is a property of THIS policy, which the .lara door pins; it is not something the
# backend enforces. What ord@1 enforces is that theory entries are UNCITABLE (design §2.2): a unit
# may declare entries and still be accepted, but no certificate can consult one.
theory sha256:ord-labeled-v1-theory-0 = []

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): the strict rules are lt_recheck and le_reflex, whose
# conclusion patterns num_lt(Bv, Sv) and num_le(Lv, Rv) head no contrary pair, so no contrary side
# is strict-reachable and the policy passes the R12 check — the same argument, and the same Lean
# lemmas, as ord-v1 and ord-named-v1.
